   "answer": "[1] Role of branched-chain amino acid metabolism in the pathogenesis of obesity and type 2 diabetes-related metabolic disturbances BCAA metabolism in type 2 diabetes (introduction)\nType 2 diabetes (T2D) is one of world\u2019s most prevalent diseases, and is related to the epidemic of obesity. Obesity can lead to the onset of T2D when pancreatic \u03b2-cells are no longer able to compensate higher insulin secretion for the reduced insulin sensitivity that often accompanies obesity. Over the last decade, branched-chain amino acids (BCAA) catabolism has increasingly been considered to have an emerging role in the development of insulin resistance in people with obesity and T2D. In these individuals, BCAA levels are considerably elevated in plasma and tissues. Furthermore, elevated BCAA levels in plasma strongly associate with insulin resistance in people with obesity and T2D. [1] Role of branched-chain amino acid metabolism in the pathogenesis of obesity and type 2 diabetes-related metabolic disturbances BCAA metabolism in type 2 diabetes (introduction)\nSeveral studies showed that plasma BCAA levels in overweight and obese humans with insulin resistance and patients with T2D were elevated compared to healthy individuals. Recently, in an observational study, we confirmed this finding and showed",
